(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 31-year-old camper develops a prickling sensation lasting about 30 minutes after wading in a freshwater lake, followed 10 to 12 hours later by severe itching and then erythematous papules. What is this condition, and what completes its life cycle?","choices":["Cercarial dermatitis; snails complete the cycle","Cutaneous larva migrans; from contaminated soil","Bedbug bites from campsite bedding","Scabies; a burrowing mite"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cercarial dermatitis, also called swimmer's itch or clam digger's itch, results from penetration of the skin by cercarial forms of parasitic flatworms. Host animals such as waterfowl and muskrats pass eggs in feces into water, the eggs hatch and infect snails within 12 hours, and cercariae are released about 5 weeks later. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman is found on routine examination to have several smooth, firm, deep red papules under 5 mm on the trunk that blanch with pressure. She has never noticed them. What education should be given?","choices":["Benign, but can progress to malignancy","They indicate liver disease","Benign capillary proliferation","Biopsy is needed to diagnose them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cherry angiomas are acquired vascular lesions formed by capillary or venule proliferation, very common and increasing with age. Treatment is unnecessary unless they bother the patient, and patients should be aware that new lesions will likely develop and there is no way to prevent them. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has widespread intensely itchy raised weals that have come and gone for four days. Individual lesions last a few hours, blanch fully, and leave no mark. She has no fever, joint pain, or bruising at resolved sites. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Urticaria","Urticarial vasculitis","Contact dermatitis","Erythema multiforme"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Urticaria results from mast cell degranulation producing transient pruritic weals. Individual lesions last less than 24 hours, blanch fully, and resolve without residual change. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with diabetes has an asymptomatic brownish patch in the crural region and scaling between the fourth and fifth toes. Under a Wood's lamp the affected skin fluoresces coral-red. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Contact dermatitis","Tinea cruris","Cutaneous candidiasis","Erythrasma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Erythrasma is a chronic superficial infection of intertriginous skin by Corynebacterium minutissimum, favouring the inner thighs, crural region, scrotum, and fourth toe web, and coral-red fluorescence under a Wood's lamp is diagnostic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has a moveable nodule on the back with a central punctum, which he refers to as a sebaceous cyst. Why is that term a misnomer for an epidermoid cyst?","choices":["It has no lining, being a pseudo-cyst","It arises from apocrine glands","It contains lipid-laden macrophages","Enclosed epithelium filled with keratin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An epidermoid cyst is formed by cystic enclosure of epithelium within the dermis and becomes filled with keratin. It is often called a sebaceous cyst because it appears to contain sebum. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with tinea corporis asks whether the infection can spread to her mouth, and a student asks why dermatophytes never involve mucous membranes. What is the correct explanation?","choices":["They survive only on dead keratin","Salivary enzymes kill them","They can involve mucosa if immunocompromised","They require sebum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dermatophytes infect and survive only on dead keratin, involving the superficial portion of the skin at the stratum corneum, plus hair and nails. They cannot survive on mucous membranes. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man of Northern European ancestry has an intensely itchy, burning eruption of grouped small vesicles and papules symmetrically over both elbows, knees, and buttocks. Most lesions are excoriated. He has autoimmune thyroid disease. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Scabies","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Atopic dermatitis","Contact dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dermatitis herpetiformis produces intensely pruritic grouped vesicles and papules symmetrically on extensor surfaces, driven by immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase in gluten-sensitive individuals. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has a biopsy-proven invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma on the dorsal forearm. What is the preferred management?","choices":["Cryotherapy","Topical imiquimod or fluorouracil","Surgical excision or Mohs surgery","Radiotherapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery is preferred for invasive squamous cell carcinoma, with at least annual skin and lymph node examination and closer intervals for high-risk or immunosuppressed patients. All suspected invasive lesions are referred to dermatology or an experienced surgeon. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 29-year-old hairdresser reports recurrent episodes of intensely itchy blisters on her hands. Examination shows multiple grouped 1 to 2 mm vesicles on the lateral aspects of the fingers and the adjacent interdigital surfaces, some coalescing, on a poorly defined pink base with surrounding dryness. She reports previous episodes that persisted for several weeks, dried up, and then peeled. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis of the hands","Allergic contact dermatitis","Nummular eczema","Dyshidrotic eczema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dyshidrotic eczema is a chronic, relapsing eruption of intensely pruritic vesicles on the hands and feet, classically described as tapioca vesicles. They may coalesce into bullae, persist for several weeks, then desiccate and resolve with desquamation — the exact course she describes. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has a basal cell carcinoma on the nasal ala that has recurred after a previous excision. When should Mohs micrographic surgery be considered for basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Only after topical therapy fails twice","High-risk sites, recurrence, or tissue sparing","Every basal cell carcinoma","Never; surgery is not required"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mohs surgery is indicated for high-risk anatomic sites, recurrent tumours, aggressive histology, or where tissue-sparing is needed. The overall goal is complete eradication with minimal cosmetic and functional deformity. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman presents with right-sided facial weakness, painful vesicles in the right ear canal, and vertigo. What treatment is added beyond antivirals?","choices":["No additional treatment","A corticosteroid instead of an antiviral","A systemic corticosteroid","Urgent ophthalmology referral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ramsay Hunt syndrome, or herpes zoster oticus, is peripheral facial palsy with painful vesicles of the ear canal, auricle, or oropharynx, sometimes with hearing loss, tinnitus, or vertigo. Antiviral therapy plus a systemic corticosteroid is started early when not contraindicated. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman whose sister developed a large keloid after ear piercing asks how to avoid the same problem. What is described as the most important element in the management of keloids?","choices":["Compression for one week after injury","Intralesional steroid alone","Early excision of every lesion","Prevention, including avoiding ear piercing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prevention is described as the most important treatment. High-risk patients should be advised to avoid cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing, and post-surgical education includes avoiding stretching of an immature scar and avoiding hot baths. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man is referred with a red, warm, tender left lower leg. He has bilateral chronic ankle swelling and brown discoloration of both gaiter regions, but the left leg has become acutely more swollen and painful over two days. He is afebrile. What consideration should most urgently shape the evaluation?","choices":["Acute unilateral swelling suggests thrombosis","Bilateral change makes cellulitis likely","Start compression immediately","No fever excludes a vascular event"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The differential for stasis dermatitis explicitly flags that acute unilateral swelling or pain should raise concern for deep venous thrombosis. Chronic bilateral changes with a new unilateral acute change is precisely that pattern. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"An 83-year-old nursing home resident with limited mobility and incontinence is at risk of pressure injury. What is the single best measure?","choices":["Restricting fluids","Prophylactic hydrocolloid dressings","Repositioning every eight hours","Prevention, with two-hourly repositioning"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The best measure is prevention: frequent skin assessments, nutrition assessment, moisture control and skin care with the skin kept clean and dry, incontinence managed and barrier creams applied, and repositioning every two hours. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with toxic epidermal necrolysis is admitted to the burn unit. What scoring system should be calculated within 24 hours and repeated on day three?","choices":["The Fitzpatrick scale","The minimal erythema dose","SCORTEN","The Parkland formula"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. SCORTEN should be calculated within 24 hours of admission and repeated on day 3. Each variable scores one point, including age over 40 years, presence of malignancy, heart rate over 120 beats per minute, and the initial extent of epidermal detachment. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a sharply circumscribed, darkly pigmented papule under 7 mm on the thigh. What is this lesion also called, and what is its typical demographic?","choices":["Blue nevus, adolescence, dorsal hands","Reed nevus, lower extremities in the thirties","Spitz nevus, children, face and neck","Dysplastic naevus, irregular borders"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pigmented spindle cell nevus, also called Reed nevus, occurs commonly in the thirties with females affected more than males, found on the extremities and mainly the lower extremities, especially the thigh. It is a sharply circumscribed darkly pigmented papule usually less than 7 mm. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 6-week-old girl born prematurely has a bright red papule on the scalp. It began as a faint blanched area with fine telangiectasias at birth and has grown noticeably over the past fortnight. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pyogenic granuloma","Infantile hemangioma","Nevus flammeus","Nevus simplex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Infantile hemangiomas are the most common tumours of infancy, more frequent in preterm and female infants with a head and neck predilection. The earliest sign is blanching followed by fine telangiectasias, then a red macule, then rapid proliferation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has a firm, bulbous nodule at the site of an ear piercing performed two years ago. The lesion extends well beyond the original piercing site and has continued to enlarge. What distinguishes this from a hypertrophic scar?","choices":["It stays within the wound margins","It is always painless","It developed within four weeks","It extends beyond the wound margins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A keloid extends beyond the wound site margins, develops slowly, continues to enlarge for months to years, and shows no regression with a tendency to recur. A hypertrophic scar develops rapidly within four weeks, remains within the wound margins, and flattens with time. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has a typical history and examination for atopic dermatitis. Her parents ask whether blood tests are needed to confirm the diagnosis. What is the most accurate response?","choices":["Biopsy every child before treatment","Check an immunoglobulin E level in every child","Culture every child at diagnosis","It is clinical; no routine laboratory testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is usually clinical, supported by family history, personal history of atopy, and a recurrent history of rash. Immunoglobulin E may be elevated but is not routinely tested, and routine laboratory testing is not required. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A newborn boy is born with a large pigmented plaque over the posterior scalp and upper back. What additional evaluation should be considered?","choices":["Observation alone with no imaging","Genetic testing of the parents","Complete excision in every case","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain and spine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Congenital melanocytic naevi arise from somatic mutations and are evident at birth or shortly after. The larger the lesion, the higher the risk for melanoma. With naevi on the head or axial locations, neurocutaneous melanosis must be considered, and magnetic resonance imaging of the brain with or without the total spine is obtained concordant with the anatomic location. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection has multiple red-purple macules and plaques on the trunk. What is the first management priority?","choices":["Start or optimise antiretroviral therapy","Start systemic chemotherapy first","Observation alone","Intralesional vincristine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral examination is essential when Kaposi sarcoma is suspected, since hard palate lesions are common and may be the presenting site. For acquired immunodeficiency syndrome-associated disease the first priority is to begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy, because immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman presents with a rash on her trunk. Ten days before the current eruption she noticed a single oval, sharply demarcated salmon-coloured plaque about 4 cm across on her upper back that became scaly and began to clear in the centre. Now numerous smaller similar lesions have appeared over the trunk, with their long axes aligned along the lines of the ribs. She feels well. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Pityriasis rosea","Guttate psoriasis","Secondary syphilis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A herald patch occurs in 50 to 90 percent of cases, preceding the exanthem: a single 2 to 5 cm round or oval, sharply demarcated pink or salmon-coloured plaque on the chest, neck, or back that clears centrally, leaving a collarette of scale. The eruption of smaller similar lesions follows 1 to 2 weeks later. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"Computed tomography of a 48-year-old man with suspected necrotizing fasciitis shows gas in the soft tissue fascial planes. What does this indicate about the likely organism?","choices":["Gas is present in every case","Gas means the infection is superficial","Gas means a fungal cause","Gas suggests Clostridium, not group A streptococcus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gas may be present with Clostridium perfringens infection but is not present with group A streptococcal infection. Ultrasound is also useful for demonstrating air bubbles in soft tissue. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 129"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with obesity has candidal intertrigo of both inframammary folds. Beyond antifungal therapy, what environmental measure is central to treatment?","choices":["Apply an occlusive ointment to the folds","Dry the folds and reduce friction and occlusion","Apply a high-potency corticosteroid","Increase the frequency of hot showers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Correcting the environment is central: gently dry the folds, reduce friction and occlusion, use moisture-wicking fabric or absorbent material, and address incontinence or hyperhidrosis. Intertrigo itself is caused by friction, moisture, and heat trapped in body folds. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 13-year-old boy has several elevated round papules under 1 cm with a rough greyish surface on the fingers. Paring one reveals tiny red-black dots. Skin lines are interrupted where the lesions sit. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Verruca vulgaris","Molluscum contagiosum","Clavus","Seborrheic keratosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Common warts are elevated round papules under 1 cm with a rough greyish surface, most often on the hands and fingers, and tiny red or black dots representing thrombosed dilated capillaries become more prominent when the surface is trimmed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 142"},{"q":"An 8-year-old girl has an itchy scalp, low-grade fever, occipital lymphadenopathy, and irritability. Wet combing recovers several moving 2 mm insects, and small oval structures are cemented to hair shafts near the scalp. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pediculosis capitis","Scabies","Tinea capitis","Seborrheic dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Head lice are blood-sucking wingless insects about 2 mm long, common in children aged 3 to 12, producing pruritus, low-grade fever, regional lymphadenopathy, and irritability. Live lice found on combing indicate active infestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 43-year-old factory worker splashes a concentrated alkali solution on the forearm, while a colleague of the same age has had weeks of soreness from repeated soap exposure. How does the timing of an irritant reaction to a strong alkali differ from that of a reaction to a mild irritant such as soap?","choices":["Alkalis act in minutes; soaps over weeks","Both act only after weeks","Alkalis over weeks; soaps in minutes","Neither acts before 48 to 72 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms present differently depending on the exposure: mild irritants such as soaps may present with subacute symptoms that progress over weeks, while alkalis and acids may present minutes after exposure or delayed up to 24 hours or more. Concentrated agents can lead to chemical burns and necrosis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has greasy yellow scale over the scalp, eyebrows, and nasolabial folds. What organism is responsible for seborrheic dermatitis, and where is the condition typically found?","choices":["An acquired dermatophyte infection","Staphylococcus aureus colonisation","Malassezia overgrowth at sebum-rich sites","Malassezia overgrowth at flexural sites"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic dermatitis is caused by overgrowth of Malassezia, which is normal skin flora. It is typically found on the scalp as dandruff, and on the eyebrows, under facial hair, in the nasolabial creases, forehead, nose, behind the ears, and in the external auditory canal. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with plaque psoriasis of the elbows and knees is prescribed calcipotriene. What is the most important clinical consideration for this agent?","choices":["Irritation and photosensitivity","Reserve it for the face and eyelids","Caution over nephrotoxicity","Risk of cutaneous atrophy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Calcipotriene is a vitamin D analogue described as having the quickest action among the first-line options, with caution regarding nephrotoxicity. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 163"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman presents with sudden onset of fever to 39.1 degrees Celsius, chills, and myalgias, followed within a day by a painful red area on her left shin. Examination shows a brightly erythematous plaque raised above the surrounding skin with a sharply defined border, and red streaks extending proximally. She has tinea pedis. What is the most likely organism and the drug of choice?","choices":["Streptococcus pyogenes, treated with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Corynebacterium minutissimum, treated with topical erythromycin","Streptococcus pyogenes, treated with penicillin V","Staphylococcus aureus, treated with cephalexin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Group A streptococcus is the most common organism in erysipelas, which involves the upper dermis and superficial cutaneous lymphatics. Penicillin V is the pharmacotherapy, with clindamycin if the patient is penicillin allergic. Tinea pedis is a listed risk factor. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 42-year-old Black woman has multiple small, smooth, firm dark brown papules 1 to 3 mm on the face and neck. What is the diagnosis, and how does it relate to seborrheic keratosis?","choices":["Melanocytic naevi","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans, a premalignant variant","Syringomas","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans, a benign variant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dermatosis papulosa nigrans consists of multiple small, smooth, firm black or dark brown papules 1 to 5 mm on the face and neck, identical to small seborrheic keratoses. It is thought to be genetic and is believed to be a developmental defect of the hair follicle. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with tender pretibial nodules undergoes a deep incisional biopsy. What finding confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Septal panniculitis without vasculitis, with Miescher's granulomas","Lobular panniculitis with vasculitis","Palisading granulomas with central necrobiosis and mucin","Full-thickness epidermal necrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy shows septal panniculitis without vasculitis and Miescher's granulomas. A deep incisional biopsy is needed because the pathology lies in the subcutaneous fat. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"An 84-year-old nursing home resident has thick flaking scale over the hands and trunk but reports no itching at all. What is this presentation?","choices":["Crusted scabies","Psoriasis","Ordinary scabies","Xerosis of aging"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkeratotic or crusted scabies presents as thick flaking scale containing millions of mites. These patients are highly infectious, and pruritus is usually absent — which is exactly why the diagnosis is missed and outbreaks occur. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has grouped papules and papulopustules around the mouth, and the clinician is distinguishing perioral dermatitis from its principal differential diagnoses. What finding would most specifically point to acne vulgaris instead?","choices":["Open or closed comedones","Scale on scalp and nasolabial folds","Papulopustules on the face","Central erythema with telangiectasia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acne vulgaris is more likely when open or closed comedones are present. Perioral dermatitis produces grouped monomorphic papules, papulovesicles, or papulopustules without comedones. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with suspected Rocky Mountain spotted fever has blood drawn before treatment is started. What laboratory abnormalities are characteristic, and what is the gold standard diagnostic test?","choices":["Hypernatraemia and hyperglycaemia","Thrombocytopenia with a normal white count","Eosinophilia with raised immunoglobulin E","Leukocytosis with thrombocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those are the characteristic laboratory findings, and the indirect immunofluorescence assay is the gold standard. Cerebrospinal fluid may show leukocytosis, moderately elevated protein, and normal glucose. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has recurrent crops of intensely itchy 1 to 2 mm vesicles on the lateral aspects of her fingers and palms. They persist for several weeks, dry up, and then peel. Between episodes her hands are dry but clear. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Irritant contact dermatitis","Scabies","Dyshidrotic eczema","Bullous pemphigoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dyshidrotic eczema produces intensely pruritic deep-seated vesicles on the palms, lateral fingers, and soles, classically described as tapioca vesicles. They persist for weeks, desiccate, and resolve with desquamation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has central facial papules and pustules, and the clinician looks for the one lesion type that would settle whether this is acne. What is the hallmark lesion of acne vulgaris, and why does it matter diagnostically?","choices":["The nodule, whose presence defines the diagnosis","The comedone, whose absence argues against acne and toward rosacea","The pustule, whose absence argues against acne","The papule, which is unique to acne among facial eruptions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Comedones are the hallmark of acne vulgaris, and the differential specifically distinguishes rosacea by papules and pustules in the middle third of the face with telangiectasias, flushing, and an absence of comedones. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has several intensely itchy, well-demarcated coin-shaped plaques 2 to 4 cm on both lower legs, with prominent scale and crusting. Each plaque is uniform throughout, with no clearing in the middle. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nummular eczema","Psoriasis","Pityriasis rosea","Tinea corporis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nummular eczema produces intensely pruritic round coin-shaped plaques 1 to 10 cm, most often on the extremities, and the plaques are uniform in appearance without central clearing. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man has hypopigmented scaly macules over the upper back and shoulders confirmed as pityriasis versicolor. What is first-line treatment, and when is systemic therapy appropriate?","choices":["Topical first line; oral if refractory","A high-potency topical corticosteroid","Oral terbinafine first line","Oral fluconazole for everyone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical therapy is first line, including ketoconazole shampoo or cream, selenium sulfide, zinc pyrithione, ciclopirox, or topical terbinafine. Systemic therapy with oral fluconazole or itraconazole is reserved for extensive, recurrent, or topical-refractory disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman is being counselled after a melanoma diagnosis and asks what most determines her outlook. What is the dominant prognostic variable?","choices":["Fitzpatrick skin type","Lesion diameter","Anatomic subtype","Breslow thickness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Survival drops sharply with increasing Breslow thickness and with nodal or distant spread. Breslow thickness is the dominant prognostic variable and must be accurately measured at the time of initial biopsy — which is why a shave biopsy that transects the base can compromise staging. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 2-week-old neonate develops blisters at sites of handling and diapering, and genetic testing is planned. Which subtype carries the greatest long-term risk of squamous cell carcinoma and requires the most intensive multidisciplinary surveillance?","choices":["Acquired epidermolysis bullosa","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa causes severe scarring, and the multidisciplinary team includes haematology and oncology specifically for squamous cell carcinoma surveillance. Scarring subtypes carry the malignancy risk. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with diabetes has three thickened, discoloured, crumbling toenails with distal onycholysis. He asks for oral terbinafine. What must be done first?","choices":["Start terbinafine empirically","Obtain a Wood's lamp examination","Confirm the fungus first","Remove the nails surgically first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fungus must be confirmed before oral therapy, since many dystrophic nails are not fungal. Testing options include potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid-Schiff stain of nail clippings, fungal culture, or polymerase chain reaction, sampling the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man develops an exaggerated sunburn-like reaction on sun-exposed skin within hours of starting a new medication. The reaction is dose-dependent and occurred on the very first exposure. What mechanism is responsible?","choices":["Phytophotodermatitis from plant contact","Photoallergy, immunologic and needing sensitisation","Phototoxicity, non-immunologic and dose-dependent","Polymorphous light eruption"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Phototoxicity is non-immunologic and dose-dependent — the drug absorbs ultraviolet energy, generating reactive oxygen species and direct cellular damage that resembles an exaggerated sunburn. Photopatch testing is the gold standard for photoallergy, the other mechanism. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A clinician estimates the likelihood of a condition in a 60-year-old woman before ordering a test, then revises that estimate once the result returns. How do pretest and posttest probability differ?","choices":["Posttest ignores the clinical picture","They are the same quantity","Pretest applies only to screening","Pretest is before the result, posttest after"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pretest probability is the likelihood of a condition before the test result is known, informed by signs and symptoms, history and risk factors, and how common the condition is in the population. Posttest probability is the likelihood after the result is known. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with a family history of melanoma has more than a hundred naevi. Several are at least 6 mm with irregular, indistinct borders and variation in colour within a single lesion. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dysplastic naevus","Malignant melanoma","Congenital melanocytic naevus","Common acquired melanocytic naevus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dysplastic melanocytic naevi are at least 5 mm with irregular indistinct borders, common in Caucasians, with family history as a risk factor. Dysplastic nevus syndrome may produce over 100 naevi by adolescence. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl has multiple 1 to 2 mm firm skin-coloured papules symmetrically distributed on the lower eyelids that appeared around puberty. What is the origin of these lesions?","choices":["Hyperplasia of sebaceous glands around a central pore","Benign neoplasms of eccrine ducts","Collections of lipid-laden macrophages","Benign neoplasms of apocrine glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Syringomas are benign neoplasms of eccrine sweat ducts. They appear at puberty and are more common in females, presenting as multiple 1 to 2 mm papules and usually asymptomatic. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A furuncle on the neck of a 37-year-old man is incised and drained, and the culture grows methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which oral regimens are appropriate?","choices":["Dicloxacillin or cephalexin","Penicillin V or amoxicillin","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, or clindamycin","Topical mupirocin alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is cultured or suspected, the oral options given are trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, and clindamycin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl with varicella is febrile and uncomfortable, and her father asks what he may give her. What analgesic caution applies?","choices":["Give systemic corticosteroids","Give antivirals instead of analgesia","Avoid aspirin; caution with ibuprofen","Avoid all analgesics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management is supportive, avoiding aspirin in children and using caution with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Early oral antivirals are considered for higher-risk patients and intravenous acyclovir for severe disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has painful grouped vesicles on an erythematous base on the buttock that have broken down into shallow ulcers. He asks whether the location tells him which virus type he has. What is the correct response?","choices":["Either type causes either site","The vesicle appearance identifies the type","Genital lesions are always type 2","The prodrome identifies the type"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Either herpes simplex virus type can cause oral or genital infection, and lesion location does not reliably determine type. Infections can occur anywhere on the skin, and typing requires a type-specific test. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with diabetes presents with an extremely painful lesion on the back of the neck. Examination shows a deep, indurated plaque with several separate openings draining pus. He has malaise, chills, and a fever of 38.4 degrees Celsius. What is the lesion?","choices":["A cutaneous abscess","A carbuncle","Folliculitis","A furuncle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A carbuncle is two or more confluent furuncles with separate heads, arising in several hair follicles, and systemic symptoms are more common with a carbuncle than a furuncle. Incision and drainage is the mainstay, supported by oral antibiotics. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man living in a shelter has linear excoriations of the back and waist, while a 6-year-old girl in the same clinic has an itchy scalp. How does the presentation of pediculosis corporis differ from that of pediculosis capitis?","choices":["Spread by head-to-head contact","It is always asymptomatic","Linear excoriations of the back and waist","Burrows in the finger webs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Body lice produce pruritus with linear excoriations primarily on the back, neck, shoulders, and waist, and post-inflammatory pigmentation in chronic cases. Head lice affect the scalp with pruritus, low-grade fever, regional lymphadenopathy, and irritability. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man of Northern European ancestry presents with an intensely pruritic, burning eruption of grouped small vesicles and papules symmetrically distributed over the elbows, knees, and buttocks. Most lesions are excoriated. He has autoimmune thyroid disease. What direct immunofluorescence finding is expected on perilesional skin?","choices":["No deposits; mites on scraping","Granular immunoglobulin A in dermal papillae","Net-like intercellular immunoglobulin G","Linear immunoglobulin G at the basement membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dermatitis herpetiformis is caused by immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase in genetically susceptible individuals consuming gluten. The immune complexes deposit in the dermal papillae, and perilesional direct immunofluorescence showing granular immunoglobulin A is the gold standard test. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has a pigmented lesion on the upper back that his wife says has changed. Examination shows an 11 mm lesion whose halves do not match, with a notched border and brown, black, and blue-grey areas within it. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dysplastic naevus","Seborrheic keratosis","Malignant melanoma","Solar lentigo"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lesion meets the ABCDE criteria: asymmetry, an irregular notched border, colour variegation with brown, black, and blue within one lesion, a diameter over 6 mm, and evolution reported by a family member. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man is diagnosed with pityriasis rosea. The eruption began four days ago and is mildly itchy. He is otherwise well and not pregnant-exposed or immunocompromised. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Reassurance, with an antihistamine for itch","Reassurance alone, no treatment for itch","Oral terbinafine","Oral acyclovir for all patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pityriasis rosea is self-limited and the mainstay is reassurance. Oral antihistamines or topical steroids can be used cautiously for pruritus, and phototherapy or natural sunlight may help if begun in the first week of the eruption. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with biopsy-confirmed erythema nodosum has bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on chest radiograph together with fever and arthralgia. What is the most appropriate referral?","choices":["Infectious disease, for probable endemic mycosis","Gastroenterology, for probable inflammatory bowel disease","Dermatology, for repeat biopsy","Pulmonology or rheumatology, for probable Löfgren syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Löfgren syndrome is the association referred to pulmonology or rheumatology. The combination of erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, fever, and arthralgia is its recognised presentation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with cellulitis of the leg has an area that is tense, cyanotic, and bronzed with blanching. What is the significance of this finding?","choices":["The expected look of resolving cellulitis","Devitalised tissue; antibiotics cannot reach it","Deep vein thrombosis; anticoagulate","Increase the dose and observe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Devitalised tissue that is tense, cyanotic, necrotic, bronzed, and blanched will not be perfused, so antibiotics will not get to the site. Surgical debridement is required. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman describes three days of burning pain in a band across her left flank, followed by an eruption. Examination shows grouped vesicles on an erythematous base confined to a single band that stops abruptly at the midline. What are the three clinical phases of this condition?","choices":["Prodromal fever, generalised eruption, desquamation","Pre-eruptive, acute eruptive, postherpetic neuralgia","Pre-eruptive, eruptive, and scarring","Incubation, contagious, and latency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pre-eruptive phase is characterised by dysesthesia or pain within the affected dermatome with lesion onset by 48 to 72 hours; the acute eruptive phase produces grouped herpetiform vesicles on an erythematous base with new lesions forming over 3 to 5 days; and postherpetic neuralgia is the chronic phase. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has several beige to dark brown papules and plaques 5 to 15 mm on the trunk that feel velvety and appear stuck onto the skin surface. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic keratoses","Malignant melanoma","Solar lentigines","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic keratoses are benign papules and plaques, beige to brown to black, 2 to 20 mm in diameter, feeling velvety or warty and appearing stuck or pasted onto the skin. They are common in older adults and are easily mistaken for neoplasms. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection has multiple red-purple macules and plaques on the trunk. Oral examination reveals similar lesions on the hard palate. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Kaposi sarcoma","Purpura","Bacillary angiomatosis","Cherry angiomas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Kaposi sarcoma develops in the cells lining blood and lymph vessels and is associated with human herpesvirus 8, producing red or purple macules, plaques, or nodules on skin or mucous membranes. Hard palate lesions are common and may be the presenting site. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has several lesions around the nose and mouth. Examination shows golden-yellow adherent crusts over shallow erosions, with palpable regional lymphadenopathy. He is afebrile and there are only four lesions. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral cephalexin","Topical mupirocin over the intact crusts","Topical mupirocin, removing crusts first","Oral penicillin V"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Topical therapy is used for a single or limited number of non-bullous lesions, and mupirocin ointment is adequate for most cases of impetigo and as effective as oral therapy with fewer side effects. Crusts should be removed before applying it. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has had erythematous scaly patches and plaques on the trunk for four years, several larger than 5 cm. They have been treated as psoriasis and eczema without lasting response, and pruritus is prominent. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma","Secondary syphilis","Eczematous dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mycosis fungoides begins in the skin and may remain confined there for years, producing localised or generalised erythematous patches or scaly plaques frequently over 5 cm on the trunk. Chronicity, treatment resistance, and large or oddly distributed lesions are the discriminators. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A clinician searches for published evidence to answer a specific question about a 65-year-old smoker with chest pain and haemoptysis, and finds none that applies. What are the limitations of applying evidence-based medicine at the bedside?","choices":["It is time consuming and evidence is often unavailable","It applies only to surgical decisions","It always answers definitively but is expensive","It cannot be combined with guidelines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The limitations given are that the process is time consuming and that many clinical questions do not have relevant evidence to answer them. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man in Connecticut develops an expanding erythematous oval lesion 8 cm across with central clearing and a darker punctate centre at the site of a recent tick bite. What stage is this?","choices":["Stage 3, late persistent infection","Stage 1, early localised infection","Stage 2, early disseminated infection","Cellulitis at the bite site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage 1 early localised infection is characterised by erythema migrans, a lesion greater than 5 cm that expands with central clearing and often a darker punctate centre, also called a bull's-eye. A patient with this lesion should be diagnosed and treated on that basis, and testing is most helpful in patients from non-endemic regions with non-diagnostic symptoms. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has mild plaque psoriasis affecting the elbows and knees. He has not used any treatment. What is the most appropriate first-line approach?","choices":["Methotrexate immediately","Emollients, topical steroids, calcipotriene","Salicylic acid and coal tar","A tapering course of oral prednisone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-line treatment of mild plaque psoriasis is emollients, topical steroids, and calcipotriene, a vitamin D analogue with the quickest action, with phototherapy using ultraviolet B best for smaller stubborn areas. Salicylic acid and coal tar are second line. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 163"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a lifetime of outdoor work has multiple rough, scaly erythematous papules on the scalp and dorsal hands, each 2 to 8 mm, that feel like sandpaper when palpated. What molecular event is the critical step in their development?","choices":["Ultraviolet-induced TP53 mutation","Hyperinsulinaemia driving keratinocyte growth","Immunoglobulin A complex deposition","Ultraviolet-induced melanocyte proliferation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses are intraepidermal keratinocytic dysplasias induced by cumulative ultraviolet B exposure, and ultraviolet-induced mutations in TP53 are the critical molecular event. They represent a field cancerisation process. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"}]};
